                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b623cf1a-721f-42da-96dd-328a6e260c00",
                        "source_entry_id": "9accd3af-cd07-3ba3-bcb9-ce578c5bea33",
                        "source_sense_id": "282dae20-34ef-3832-adc1-e0ef34a7afd6",
                        "target_lang_word": "novemdecillion",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "novemdecillion",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4807a1cf-d413-36df-9421-2377ece1d123",
                                        "entry_id": "6b8978e3-c075-3be3-978b-ec55e842eec8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "10⁶⁰."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US",
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "afc9d7fd-6ca8-4834-8a76-a117c80d4269",
                                                "sense_id": "4807a1cf-d413-36df-9421-2377ece1d123",
                                                "text": "It is one chance in a novemdecillion. For those who like to be precise, that exact statistic is one chance in 1,606,937,974,174,171,729,761,809,705,564,167,968,221,676,069,604,401,795,301,376."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a21f1fa5-f6a0-4a01-bd98-ceac6fad65d9",
                                                "sense_id": "4807a1cf-d413-36df-9421-2377ece1d123",
                                                "text": "For an extreme example, see US Patent No. 5,422,351 (filed June 21, 1991). This particular patent includes a structural formula in claim 1 that encompasses at least one novemdecillion (10r^([sic]), or one followed by sixty zeroes) chemical compounds"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b2fc59cc-e9d6-4996-8f8c-dc98dbb46996",
                                                "sense_id": "4807a1cf-d413-36df-9421-2377ece1d123",
                                                "text": "It has the shape of a disk, and is ten to the fifty-ninth power yojanas in circumference, a number called a novemdecillion, and is 1,600,000 yojanas deep."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4a37d599-df35-4628-bc9a-4d5c571766eb",
                                                "sense_id": "4807a1cf-d413-36df-9421-2377ece1d123",
                                                "text": "Go up to 60 zeros -- that's a novemdecillion -- and you can measure the volume of the galaxy in cubic inches [...]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "03e10ba5-8c17-4f16-b736-cdb536ef46e1",
                                                "sense_id": "4807a1cf-d413-36df-9421-2377ece1d123",
                                                "text": "The index tells us that Zimbabwe's inflation rate recently peaked at 80 billion percent a month. That means around 6.5 quindecillion novemdecillion percent a year—or 65 followed by 107 zeros."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "18dd153d-9431-49be-99a2-5dd060bf87e6",
                                                "sense_id": "4807a1cf-d413-36df-9421-2377ece1d123",
                                                "text": "grangs med, literally “without count,” is also said to indicate the number ten to the fiftieth or sixtieth power (novemdecillion). Still less than a googol!"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a18bb81f-673f-37ef-8110-52a3c382089d",
                                        "entry_id": "6b8978e3-c075-3be3-978b-ec55e842eec8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "10¹¹⁴."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "dated",
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2fdb51de-4b6f-3e41-997c-ce2d1e60e8d6",
                                        "entry_id": "6b8978e3-c075-3be3-978b-ec55e842eec8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A very large but unspecified number (of)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "colloquial",
                                            "excessive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "15b69e30-221f-45a1-96c3-97281c44cd95",
                                                "sense_id": "2fdb51de-4b6f-3e41-997c-ce2d1e60e8d6",
                                                "text": "2000–2006: Quantum Mechanics, Abarim Publications http://www.abarim-publications.com/JessicaRabbitExplains.html\n‘When we say 2 we mean exactly 2, not 2,00001 or 2,0000000000000001 or 2 with a novemdecillion zeroes and then a 1...’."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3b8d4216-251a-410e-855f-801e9e9fb4a5",
                                                "sense_id": "2fdb51de-4b6f-3e41-997c-ce2d1e60e8d6",
                                                "text": "‘I then looked into the zatetic forest behind it / And saw a nonillion, no, a novemdecillion of them!’."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6a4aa52e-58d9-41e7-94de-b403d8373319",
                                                "sense_id": "2fdb51de-4b6f-3e41-997c-ce2d1e60e8d6",
                                                "text": "‘The odds that one of the Cowboys linebacking corps reads this blog is one in... oh, let’s use a really big number... a novemdecillion’."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
